<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shina-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shina language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shina is a Dardic Indo-Aryan language spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-baltistan">Gilgit-Baltistan</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and in neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Kohistan. It is the main language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> and of several valleys, including parts of Nagar, and has many dialects. Long an unwritten language, it has been written in a modified Arabic-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> script since the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shina-language.jpg" alt="Shina language" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThatDohDude · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shina_in_Nastaliq.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
